= Hindsight bias =

== Overview ==
'''Hindsight bias''' is the tendency, after learning an outcome, to judge that the outcome was more predictable or expected than it appeared beforehand.

== Components ==
Research distinguishes memory distortion, perceived inevitability, and perceived foreseeability. These components need not move together.

== Epistemic importance ==
Hindsight can distort evaluation of past decisions by replacing the uncertainty available at decision time with knowledge available afterward.

== References ==
* Baruch Fischhoff, “Hindsight ≠ Foresight: The Effect of Outcome Knowledge on Judgment Under Uncertainty”, ''Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance'' 1 (1975): 288–299.
* Roese & Vohs, hindsight-bias review literature.

